<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
    Loopback-only Fast-DDS profile for the test suite.

    Provizio's self-hosted CI runners share a local network, and the test suite
    creates participants on the default domain (0) with a small set of fixed
    topic names. With the default transport a participant binds to every host
    interface (verified: 0.0.0.0 plus the LAN/overlay addresses), so two runners
    executing the same suite concurrently discover each other and cross-deliver
    samples. Tests that integrate every sample they receive — point-cloud
    accumulation in particular — then read another runner's data as if it were
    their own and fail non-deterministically.

    Confining every participant to the loopback interface makes cross-host
    discovery and data exchange impossible while leaving same-host communication
    (the only thing the tests need) intact:
      - interfaceWhiteList 127.0.0.1 binds all locators to loopback only.
      - useBuiltinTransports=false swaps the default SHM+UDP stack for the single
        whitelisted UDPv4 transport (also sidestepping platforms where shared
        memory is unavailable).
      - initialPeersList 127.0.0.1 drives unicast discovery to localhost, since
        the loopback interface carries no multicast on Linux.
      - maxInitialPeersRange 50 widens how many participant indices that unicast
        initial peer probes. An initial peer given as an address with no port
        expands to the well-known metatraffic ports of participant indices
        [0, maxInitialPeersRange); Fast-DDS's default is 4, so only the first four
        participants on a domain are reachable this way — and with multicast off
        (loopback) a test that creates a fifth-or-later participant then sees it
        silently fail to discover its peers: a matching timeout that flakes with
        participant-id assignment order. 50 comfortably exceeds the participant
        count of any single test while staying cheap on loopback. (This only bites
        because we force unicast-only discovery; the default transport would reach
        the rest via multicast.)

    Applied via FASTDDS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE in test.sh. The library defers
    entirely to this profile when the variable is set, so its own transport and
    discovery tuning is intentionally skipped (see domain_participant.cpp). Tests
    that exercise that code path (discovery_tuning) unset the variable
    themselves.
-->
<profiles xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com">
    <transport_descriptors>
        <transport_descriptor>
            <transport_id>test_loopback_udpv4</transport_id>
            <type>UDPv4</type>
            <interfaceWhiteList>
                <address>127.0.0.1</address>
            </interfaceWhiteList>
            <!-- Probe participant indices [0, 50) via the unicast initial peer
                 below (default 4 is too few once a test creates >4 participants
                 on a domain with multicast off). See the header comment. -->
            <maxInitialPeersRange>50</maxInitialPeersRange>
        </transport_descriptor>
    </transport_descriptors>
    <participant profile_name="provizio_test_loopback" is_default_profile="true">
        <rtps>
            <userTransports>
                <transport_id>test_loopback_udpv4</transport_id>
            </userTransports>
            <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
            <builtin>
                <initialPeersList>
                    <locator>
                        <udpv4>
                            <address>127.0.0.1</address>
                        </udpv4>
                    </locator>
                </initialPeersList>
            </builtin>
        </rtps>
    </participant>
</profiles>
